$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: usm/feedback-upstream-routing
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-08-13"
summary: >-
  Scope-aware feedback routing — the generated feedback protocol, MCP tool, and docs page
  distinguish bugs in the consuming project (route per project policy/tracker) from bugs in the USM
  tool itself (route upstream), with the upstream URL literal and override. When the project tracker
  and upstream tracker are the same place (the USM repo itself), the protocol collapses to coherent
  single-tracker text instead of contradicting itself.
$system: usm/system
$service: usm/cli
intent: >-
  Agents in downstream projects misattribute USM tool bugs to the project they are working in. The
  generated feedback protocol says 'Real bugs live in the issue tracker: <this repo>' with no scope
  distinction, so an agent hitting an update_feature data-loss bug files (or gates) it against
  daingerdainger-com instead of the USM repo. Tool bugs need explicit upstream routing with the
  exact target.
status: built
flows:
  - id: agent-classifies-scope
    name: Agent classifies a bug by blast radius
    description: >-
      When an agent discovers a bug, it first determines whether the fault is in the project or in
      the USM tool itself.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: detect
        target: bug, inconsistency, or wrong generator/MCP output
      - id: s2
        action: classify
        target: >-
          USM tool itself (CLI commands, MCP tool behaviour, generator output, schema validation) vs
          this project (app code, infra, this repo's .usm specs)
      - id: s3
        action: branch
        target: project scope → existing feedback policy; USM-tool scope → upstream routing
  - id: route-upstream
    name: Route a USM tool bug upstream
    description: >-
      USM tool bugs are drafted and filed against the upstream tracker with version and environment
      context.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: >-
          upstream tracker URL — feedback.upstream_tracker, default
          https://github.com/Smith-Gray-Pty-Ltd/usm/issues
      - id: s2
        action: draft
        target: "issue with: @smithgray/usm version, command/tool invoked, repro, expected vs actual"
      - id: s3
        action: respect-policy
        target: >-
          human-gate: ask the human before filing; direct-to-github: gh issue create -R
          Smith-Gray-Pty-Ltd/usm
      - id: s4
        action: forbidden
        target: never file USM tool bugs in the consuming project's tracker or .usm/feedback
  - id: render-scope-aware-protocol
    name: Render the scope-aware protocol everywhere
    description: >-
      The Where does the bug live? guidance is emitted into every agent-facing surface from one code
      path.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: generate
        target: >-
          generateFeedbackProtocol gains a scope table: project scope vs USM tool scope with
          respective destinations
      - id: s2
        action: render
        target: >-
          all rules files (AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, .cursor/rules/usm.mdc, copilot, opencode skill) via
          the shared block
      - id: s3
        action: render
        target: docs feedback page (generateFeedbackPage) gains the same scope table
  - id: mcp-tool-explicit
    name: MCP tool carries the distinction
    description: usm_report_feedback surfaces the scope question at the tool boundary.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: update
        target: >-
          tool description: distinguishes project feedback from USM tool bugs; names the upstream
          repo
      - id: s2
        action: update
        target: >-
          human-gate draft response: if the entry looks like a USM tool bug, suggests routing
          upstream instead
contracts:
  - id: scope-branch-explicit
    description: >-
      Every generated feedback protocol explicitly branches on bug scope before stating where it
      goes
    must_have:
      - A 'Where does the bug live?' distinction rendered in every rules file
      - "Project scope: existing policy behaviour unchanged"
      - "USM-tool scope: upstream URL named literally with the gh -R command"
  - id: upstream-default-and-override
    description: The upstream tracker has a default and a system.feedback override
    must_have:
      - "Default upstream: https://github.com/Smith-Gray-Pty-Ltd/usm/issues"
      - Optional feedback.upstream_tracker field in system.usm overrides it
      - Schema update is optional-field-only (no breaking change)
  - id: no-misfiling
    description: The protocol must forbid filing USM tool bugs against the consuming project
    must_have:
      - Explicit 'never file USM tool bugs in this repo's tracker' rule
      - usm_report_feedback human-gate draft mentions upstream routing for tool bugs
  - id: one-source-many-surfaces
    description: Scope routing text is generated from a single code path into all surfaces
    must_have:
      - rulesFiles.ts generateFeedbackProtocol is the single source
      - Docs feedback page and MCP tool text stay consistent with it
tests:
  - id: protocol-renders-both-scopes
    setup:
      system_usm_with_feedback_block: true
      run_generate: true
    expect:
      - assertion: generated AGENTS.md contains the scope distinction
      - assertion: upstream URL literal present
      - assertion: project-scope behaviour unchanged from prior policy text
  - id: upstream-override-honoured
    setup:
      feedback_upstream_tracker: https://github.com/fork/usm/issues
    expect:
      - assertion: generated protocol uses the override URL
  - id: mcp-description-explicit
    setup:
      read_mcp_tool_registration: true
    expect:
      - assertion: usm_report_feedback description distinguishes project vs USM tool bugs
      - assertion: upstream repo named in the description
  - id: schema-accepts-override
    setup:
      system_usm_with_upstream_tracker: true
    expect:
      - assertion: usm validate passes
      - assertion: absent field still validates (optional)
decisions:
  - id: default-upstream-hardcoded
    decision: Default upstream tracker is the canonical USM repo, overridable via feedback.upstream_tracker
    rationale: >-
      Consumers should not need configuration to get correct routing; forks/rebrands can override.
      Optional schema field only — no breaking change.
  - id: classification-instructions-not-detection
    decision: Teach scope classification via instructions rather than automatic detection in the MCP tool
    rationale: >-
      Classification requires judgment about blast radius; the agent (or human at the gate) is
      better placed than a heuristic on the summary string. The tool only nudges when a USM tool bug
      seems likely.
implementation:
  primary: src/generators/rulesFiles.ts
  test_code: tests/opencodeFeedback.test.ts
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - usm/agent-feedback
  - usm/gen-rules-files
  - usm/opencode-integration
